# Environment: Claude Code native on Termux/Android

_This section is installed and kept in sync by [claude-code-termux-native](https://github.com/jronminh/claude_code_termux_native)'s `install.sh`, along with the `termux-doctor` skill it installs alongside it. A future install/update overwrites everything between the `claude-code-termux-native:begin`/`:end` markers, so don't hand-edit inside them — fix `scripts/*.sh`, `CLAUDE.md.template`, or `skills/termux-doctor/SKILL.md` in that repo instead, then re-run `install.sh`. Notes that don't belong there (drift you've observed but haven't turned into a proper fix yet) belong outside the markers, elsewhere in this file._

You are NOT running on a regular Linux system. You are Anthropic's official
`linux-arm64` glibc binary, with its ELF interpreter patched so it runs on
Android (Bionic libc) via Termux's glibc-runner. Anthropic does not publish
an `android-arm64` build; this is a community workaround, not official
support. Consequence: `process.platform` may report `linux` or `android` —
don't branch any important decision on that alone.

If claude, grep, bash, find, or an update misbehaves in ANY of these ways —
segfault on launch, "bad ELF magic", "invalid ELF header", "cannot
execute", grep/`-G` shared-library errors, `LD_PRELOAD`/`LD_LIBRARY_PATH`
weirdness, `patchelf`/glibc questions, "native binary not installed" — or
before hand-editing anything under `~/.claude/claude-native/` or
`$PREFIX/bin/claude`, **invoke the `termux-doctor` skill first**. It has
the full trap list (9 documented failure modes), the self-repair design,
and the golden rules for fixing this safely without guessing from first
principles.

Quick sanity check anytime, without invoking the skill:
`bash ~/.claude/claude-native/doctor.sh`

`install.sh` also wires that check into a `SessionStart` hook (see
`~/.claude/settings.json`), so it already ran once automatically at the
start of this session — silent if clean. If it found a problem, its full
output was injected into this session's context already; don't re-run it
speculatively, just act on what it reported (or invoke the skill).

That same hook also recognizes when the `claude` binary itself is a
different build than the one running last session (e.g. after
`termux-update-claude`): its output carries a line starting `UPDATED:`
naming the previous version. If you see that in this session's start
context, mention the version change to the user in your first reply
(old → new) — that's the whole point of the check, don't stay silent
about it just because it isn't an error. `doctor.sh` also keeps the last
two observed `claude --version` strings in
`~/.claude/claude-native/.last-claude-version` (previous, then current)
if you need to double check.

You also have a few Android-native bridges available directly as shell
commands (all from `termux-api`, needs the Termux:API app installed and
granted — `command -v termux-notification` is a quick way to check):

- **`termux-open <path-or-url>`** — opens a file or URL in whatever Android
  app the user has set as the default handler for it (e.g. a PDF viewer, an
  image viewer, a browser). Use this when the user asks to view/open
  something you just produced (a report, a generated image, a rendered
  file) rather than just naming the path and leaving it to them.
- **`termux-share <path>`** — opens Android's native share sheet for a
  file, so the user can send it to another app (Files, a messaging app,
  email, ...) without leaving Termux. Use this when the user asks to
  share/send a file somewhere, or when handing off a finished artifact
  makes more sense as a share action than just printing its path.
- Both are fire-and-forget from your side (they hand off to Android and
  return immediately) — no output to parse, just call them and mention
  what you did.
- **`termux-claude-job`** — schedules a headless `claude -p "<prompt>"` run
  through Android's real JobScheduler (`add`/`list`/`remove`/`run`/`log`;
  `termux-claude-job --help` for the full syntax), not plain cron — a
  background loop/cron job gets killed by Android the moment the screen
  locks, but a JobScheduler-backed one actually wakes the device. Suggest
  this when the user wants something to run periodically/unattended on
  this device (e.g. "check X every 15 minutes", "run the test suite
  nightly") rather than proposing a `loop`/`schedule`-skill approach that
  assumes the session stays open and awake.
- If a `UserPromptSubmit` hook reports the device's battery is low and not
  charging (opt-in, only present if `--with-notifications` was used at
  install), take it as real signal to prefer batching work over kicking off
  long unattended background tasks (including `termux-claude-job` runs)
  until it's charging — not something to just acknowledge and ignore.
- **`termux-claude-features status`** — lists every optional capability
  this setup can toggle (what each does, whether it's currently on, and
  any safety notes) in its own output — e.g. background notifications, or
  seeing/driving the actual Android screen for debugging something
  `termux-api` can't reach. Features get added here over time without
  this file changing, so run this (not memory of an earlier session)
  whenever a task might benefit from a capability beyond bare Termux,
  rather than assuming you already know the full list. `enable
  <feature>`/`disable <feature>` toggles one on/off — ask the user first
  if `status` marks that feature security-sensitive.
- Deeper technical detail on how this whole setup fits together (every
  staged script's role, the pattern behind opt-in features, why some
  things are staged vs. installed vs. merged into `~/.claude/`) lives at
  `~/.claude/claude-native/docs/architecture.md` — read it on demand when
  actually working on this setup itself (not needed for ordinary use).
  This file is deliberately kept short instead of growing with every new
  feature; `docs/architecture.md` and `termux-claude-features status`
  absorb that detail instead.
